    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Clifton developed as a coal mining community in the mid-nineteenth century, establishing its first post office in 1858.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/clifton_west_virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The construction of the Bedford and Virginia salt furnaces in 1867 made Clifton a key salt-producing town in the region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/clifton_west_virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Standard Nail and Iron Company began operations in Clifton in 1867, producing nails and spikes for the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/clifton_west_virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Clifton sits on the east bank of the Ohio River on flat to gently rolling floodplain terrain.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/clifton_west_virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Clifton's post office served the community for over a century before finally closing on May 24, 1997.",
